@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1714 = private unnamed_addr constant [1086 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for FastAPI routes that use the optional `response_model` parameter\0Awith the same type as the return type.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AFastAPI routes automatically infer the response model type from the return\0Atype, so specifying it explicitly is redundant.\0A\0AThe `response_model` parameter is used to override the default response\0Amodel type. For example, `response_model` can be used to specify that\0Aa non-serializable response type should instead be serialized via an\0Aalternative type.\0A\0AFor more information, see the [FastAPI documentation](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/response-model/).\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom fastapi import FastAPI\0Afrom pydantic import BaseModel\0A\0Aapp = FastAPI()\0A\0A\0Aclass Item(BaseModel):\0A    name: str\0A\0A\0A@app.post(\22/items/\22, response_model=Item)\0Aasync def create_item(item: Item) -> Item:\0A    return item\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom fastapi import FastAPI\0Afrom pydantic import BaseModel\0A\0Aapp = FastAPI()\0A\0A\0Aclass Item(BaseModel):\0A    name: str\0A\0A\0A@app.post(\22/items/\22)\0Aasync def create_item(item: Item) -> Item:\0A    return item\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1715 = private unnamed_addr constant [54 x i8] c"FastAPI route with redundant `response_model` argument", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1716 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1715, [8 x i8] c"6\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1717 = private unnamed_addr constant [697 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `six.PY3`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`six.PY3` will evaluate to `False` on Python 4 and greater. This is likely\0Aunintended, and may cause code intended to run on Python 2 to run on Python 4\0Atoo.\0A\0AInstead, use `not six.PY2` to validate that the current Python major version is\0A_not_ equal to 2, to future-proof the code.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport six\0A\0Asix.PY3  # `False` on Python 4.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport six\0A\0Anot six.PY2  # `True` on Python 4.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [PyPI: `six`](https://pypi.org/project/six/)\0A- [Six documentation: `six.PY2`](https://six.readthedocs.io/#six.PY2)\0A- [Six documentation: `six.PY3`](https://six.readthedocs.io/#six.PY3)\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1718 = private unnamed_addr constant [49 x i8] c"`six.PY3` referenced (python4), use `not six.PY2`", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1719 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1718, [8 x i8] c"1\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1720 = private unnamed_addr constant [760 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that async functions do not create subprocesses with blocking methods.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABlocking an async function via a blocking call will block the entire\0Aevent loop, preventing it from executing other tasks while waiting for the\0Acall to complete, negating the benefits of asynchronous programming.\0A\0AInstead of making a blocking call, use an equivalent asynchronous library\0Aor function, like [`trio.run_process()`](https://trio.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference-io.html#trio.run_process)\0Aor [`anyio.run_process()`](https://anyio.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html#anyio.run_process).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aasync def foo():\0A    os.popen(cmd)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aasync def foo():\0A    asyncio.create_subprocess_shell(cmd)\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1721 = private unnamed_addr constant [68 x i8] c"Async functions should not create subprocesses with blocking methods", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1722 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1721, [8 x i8] c"D\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1723 = private unnamed_addr constant [760 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that async functions do not run processes with blocking methods.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABlocking an async function via a blocking call will block the entire\0Aevent loop, preventing it from executing other tasks while waiting for the\0Acall to complete, negating the benefits of asynchronous programming.\0A\0AInstead of making a blocking call, use an equivalent asynchronous library\0Aor function, like [`trio.run_process()`](https://trio.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference-io.html#trio.run_process)\0Aor [`anyio.run_process()`](https://anyio.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html#anyio.run_process).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aasync def foo():\0A    subprocess.run(cmd)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aasync def foo():\0A    asyncio.create_subprocess_shell(cmd)\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1724 = private unnamed_addr constant [62 x i8] c"Async functions should not run processes with blocking methods", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1725 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1724, [8 x i8] c">\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1726 = private unnamed_addr constant [854 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that async functions do not wait on processes with blocking methods.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABlocking an async function via a blocking call will block the entire\0Aevent loop, preventing it from executing other tasks while waiting for the\0Acall to complete, negating the benefits of asynchronous programming.\0A\0AInstead of making a blocking call, use an equivalent asynchronous library\0Aor function, like [`trio.to_thread.run_sync()`](https://trio.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reference-core.html#trio.to_thread.run_sync)\0Aor [`anyio.to_thread.run_sync()`](https://anyio.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html#anyio.to_thread.run_sync).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aasync def foo():\0A    os.waitpid(0)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef wait_for_process():\0A    os.waitpid(0)\0A\0A\0Aasync def foo():\0A    await asyncio.loop.run_in_executor(None, wait_for_process)\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1727 = private unnamed_addr constant [66 x i8] c"Async functions should not wait on processes with blocking methods", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1728 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1727, [8 x i8] c"B\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1729 = private unnamed_addr constant [1003 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for timeout context managers which do not contain a checkpoint.\0A\0AFor the purposes of this check, `yield` is considered a checkpoint,\0Asince checkpoints may occur in the caller to which we yield.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASome asynchronous context managers, such as `asyncio.timeout` and\0A`trio.move_on_after`, have no effect unless they contain a checkpoint.\0AThe use of such context managers without an `await`, `async with` or\0A`async for` statement is likely a mistake.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aasync def func():\0A    async with asyncio.timeout(2):\0A        do_something()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aasync def func():\0A    async with asyncio.timeout(2):\0A        do_something()\0A        await awaitable()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`asyncio` timeouts](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-task.html#timeouts)\0A- [`anyio` timeouts](https://anyio.readthedocs.io/en/stable/cancellation.html)\0A- [`trio` timeouts](https://trio.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference-core.html#cancellation-and-timeouts)\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1730 = private unnamed_addr constant [8 x i8] c"A `with ", align 1
