@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2098 = private unnamed_addr constant [1803 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for mutable default values in class attributes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AMutable default values share state across all instances of the class,\0Awhile not being obvious. This can lead to bugs when the attributes are\0Achanged in one instance, as those changes will unexpectedly affect all\0Aother instances.\0A\0AGenerally speaking, you probably want to avoid having mutable default\0Avalues in the class body at all; instead, these variables should usually\0Abe initialized in `__init__`. However, other possible fixes for the issue\0Acan include:\0A- Explicitly annotating the variable with [`typing.ClassVar`][ClassVar] to\0A  indicate that it is intended to be shared across all instances.\0A- Using an immutable data type (e.g. a tuple instead of a list)\0A  for the default value.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass A:\0A    variable_1: list[int] = []\0A    variable_2: set[int] = set()\0A    variable_3: dict[str, int] = {}\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass A:\0A    def __init__(self) -> None:\0A        self.variable_1: list[int] = []\0A        self.variable_2: set[int] = set()\0A        self.variable_3: dict[str, int] = {}\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom typing import ClassVar\0A\0A\0Aclass A:\0A    variable_1: ClassVar[list[int]] = []\0A    variable_2: ClassVar[set[int]] = set()\0A    variable_3: ClassVar[dict[str, int]] = {}\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass A:\0A    variable_1: list[int] | None = None\0A    variable_2: set[int] | None = None\0A    variable_3: dict[str, int] | None = None\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom collections.abc import Sequence, Mapping, Set as AbstractSet\0Afrom types import MappingProxyType\0A\0A\0Aclass A:\0A    variable_1: Sequence[int] = ()\0A    variable_2: AbstractSet[int] = frozenset()\0A    variable_3: Mapping[str, int] = MappingProxyType({})\0A```\0A\0A[ClassVar]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.ClassVar\0A", align 1
